steps.body:
A page that shows fresh information without reloading, like new messages, search results as you type, or today's weather, asks a server for data and puts it on the page itself. That's what `fetch` does, and after the DOM it's the browser feature you'll use most. It also brings one new idea into your JavaScript: how code waits for something slow without freezing the page.

## A server to talk to

You need a server that answers with data. The first example below is a small one, written in JavaScript for Node.js, which came with Hack University. In the Playground, make a folder called `notes-app`, save the example in it as `server.mjs`, and make a folder called `public` beside it for your pages. The `.mjs` ending tells Node.js the file is a module, like `type="module"` in a page. Then, in the terminal, go into `notes-app` and run `node server.mjs`. Stop Python's server first if it's running, since both use port 8000.

You don't need to follow the server's code. The backend courses teach how servers like it work. What matters here is what it answers. It sends the files in `public`, so `http://localhost:8000/` is `public/index.html`. And it has an **API**, the word from the lesson on how a browser draws a page for what one program offers another. A server's API is a set of addresses meant for programs rather than people, which answer with data instead of pages. `GET /api/notes` answers with the list of notes, and `POST /api/notes` saves a new one.

## JSON

Data travels as text, and the usual format is **JSON**, short for JavaScript Object Notation. It looks like JavaScript's objects and arrays, with stricter rules: property names and strings go in double quotes, and there are no comments, no functions, and no comma after the last item. Here's the notes API's answer, asked with curl:

```bash
curl -i http://localhost:8000/api/notes
```

```text
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 22:32:06 GMT
Connection: keep-alive
Keep-Alive: timeout=5
Transfer-Encoding: chunked

[{"id":1,"text":"Bring water"},{"id":2,"text":"Check the weather"}]
```

`Content-Type: application/json`, from the How the Web Works lessons, says the body is JSON. The other headers are details of the connection. `JSON.parse(text)` turns JSON text into JavaScript values, and `JSON.stringify(value)` turns values into JSON text, which is how you'll send data back.

## Waiting without freezing

A request takes time: the DNS lookup, the connection, the server's work, and the trip back, anything from a few milliseconds to several seconds on a weak phone signal. The previous lesson showed that the main thread does one thing at a time. If `fetch` waited for the answer before returning, the page would freeze for all of that.

So `fetch` returns at once, with a **promise**: an object that stands for a result that isn't ready yet, like the buzzer a café hands you when you order. Later the promise is **fulfilled** with the result, or **rejected** with an error if it failed. Meanwhile, the page carries on.

## async and await

`await` waits for a promise and gives you its result:

```javascript
async function loadNotes() {
  const response = await fetch("/api/notes");
  const notes = await response.json();
}
```

`await` only works inside a function marked `async`. When the function reaches an `await`, it pauses there and gives the main thread back, so the browser can paint and respond. When the promise is fulfilled, the function carries on from that line, as a microtask from the previous lesson. The code reads top to bottom as if it waited, but nothing froze. Calling an `async` function starts its work and returns a promise straight away, so the line after the call runs before the function finishes. Older code uses `.then()` instead, as in `fetch(url).then((response) => response.json())`, which does the same job.

## The response

The first `await` gives you a **Response** object as soon as the status line and headers arrive. `response.status` is the status code, `response.headers.get("Content-Type")` reads a header, and `response.ok` is `true` for any status from 200 to 299. The body may still be on its way, so reading it is a second promise: `await response.json()` waits for all of it and parses it as JSON, and `await response.text()` gives it as plain text.

## When things go wrong

Here's the surprise: a 404 or a 500 doesn't make `fetch` fail. The server answered, so the promise is fulfilled, with a response whose `ok` is `false`. `fetch` only rejects when no usable answer arrived: the network is down, the name didn't resolve, or the browser blocked the response, as part 3 explains. So check `response.ok` yourself.

`try` and `catch` handle errors. The code in the `try` block runs, and if anything in it fails, a rejected `await` included, the rest of the block is skipped and the `catch` block runs, with the error. `throw new Error("...")` makes an error of your own, which is how a bad status becomes one:

```javascript
if (!response.ok) {
  throw new Error(`The server answered ${response.status}`);
}
```

## Loading and error states

A request has three moments a person sees: waiting, done, and failed. Show each one. Put up a loading message before the request, replace it with the data, and when it fails, say so in plain words. A page that shows nothing while it waits looks broken. Here's the whole `public/app.js` for a page with a `.message` paragraph and a `.notes` list:

```javascript
const list = document.querySelector(".notes");
const message = document.querySelector(".message");

async function loadNotes() {
  message.textContent = "Loading your notes...";
  try {
    const response = await fetch("/api/notes");
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`The server answered ${response.status}`);
    }
    const notes = await response.json();
    list.replaceChildren();
    for (const note of notes) {
      const item = document.createElement("li");
      item.textContent = note.text;
      list.append(item);
    }
    message.textContent = "";
  } catch (error) {
    message.textContent = "Couldn't load your notes. Try again in a moment.";
    console.error(error);
  }
}

loadNotes();
```

`console.error` prints the error in red in the Console, for you, while the page tells the reader what happened.

```diagram fetch-storage-web-apis-fetch-flow
Two waits: one for the response to arrive, one for its body. Then the page changes.
```

In the diagram, step 1 sends the request and gets a promise. In step 2, the status and headers arrive as a Response. In step 3, `json()` waits for the body and parses it. In step 4, your code puts the notes on the page.

examples.code:
import { createServer } from "node:http";
import { readFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import { extname } from "node:path";

const notes = [
  { id: 1, text: "Bring water" },
  { id: 2, text: "Check the weather" },
];

const types = { ".html": "text/html", ".js": "text/javascript", ".css": "text/css" };

function sendJson(response, status, data) {
  response.writeHead(status, { "Content-Type": "application/json" });
  response.end(JSON.stringify(data));
}

async function readJson(request) {
  let body = "";
  for await (const chunk of request) {
    body += chunk;
  }
  return JSON.parse(body);
}

const server = createServer(async (request, response) => {
  const { pathname, searchParams } = new URL(request.url, "http://localhost");
  console.log(request.method, pathname);

  if (pathname === "/api/notes" && request.method === "GET") {
    const search = (searchParams.get("q") ?? "").toLowerCase();
    return sendJson(response, 200, notes.filter((note) => note.text.toLowerCase().includes(search)));
  }

  if (pathname === "/api/notes" && request.method === "POST") {
    try {
      const { text } = await readJson(request);
      if (!text) {
        return sendJson(response, 400, { error: "A note needs some text" });
      }
      const note = { id: notes.length + 1, text: text };
      notes.push(note);
      return sendJson(response, 201, note);
    } catch {
      return sendJson(response, 400, { error: "Send the note as JSON" });
    }
  }

  try {
    const file = await readFile(`public${pathname === "/" ? "/index.html" : pathname}`);
    response.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": types[extname(pathname)] ?? "text/html" });
    response.end(file);
  } catch {
    sendJson(response, 404, { error: "Not found" });
  }
});

server.listen(8000, "127.0.0.1", () => {
  console.log("Notes server on http://localhost:8000");
});

async function showNotes() {
  const response = await fetch("/api/notes");
  console.log(response.status, response.ok);
  console.log(response.headers.get("Content-Type"));
  const notes = await response.json();
  console.log(notes.length, notes[0].text);
}

showNotes();
console.log("The request has started, and the script carries on.");

async function loadNote(id) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/notes/${id}`);
    console.log(response.status, response.ok);
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`The server answered ${response.status}`);
    }
    const note = await response.json();
    console.log(note.text);
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(`Couldn't load note ${id}: ${error.message}`);
  }
}

loadNote(99);

async function addNote(text) {
  const response = await fetch("/api/notes", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify({ text: text }),
  });
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`The server answered ${response.status}`);
  }
  return response.json();
}

async function main() {
  const note = await addNote("Pack snacks");
  console.log(`Saved note ${note.id}: ${note.text}`);

  try {
    await addNote("");
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(error.message);
  }
}

main();

const form = document.querySelector(".new-note");
const button = form.querySelector("button");
const message = document.querySelector(".message");

form.addEventListener("submit", async (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  const text = new FormData(form).get("text");
  button.disabled = true;
  message.textContent = "Saving...";
  try {
    const response = await fetch("/api/notes", {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      body: JSON.stringify({ text: text }),
    });
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`The server answered ${response.status}`);
    }
    const note = await response.json();
    message.textContent = `Saved: ${note.text}`;
    form.reset();
  } catch (error) {
    message.textContent = "Not saved. Check your connection and try again.";
  } finally {
    button.disabled = false;
  }
  console.log(message.textContent);
});

let controller = null;

async function search(query) {
  if (controller) {
    controller.abort();
  }
  controller = new AbortController();
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/notes?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`, {
      signal: controller.signal,
    });
    const notes = await response.json();
    console.log(`Notes matching ${query}: ${notes.length}`);
  } catch (error) {
    if (error.name === "AbortError") {
      console.log(`Cancelled the search for ${query}`);
      return;
    }
    throw error;
  }
}

search("wat");
search("water");

async function loadNotes() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch("http://localhost:8000/api/notes");
    const notes = await response.json();
    console.log(`Loaded ${notes.length} notes`);
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(`${error.name}: ${error.message}`);
  }
}

loadNotes();

const allowedOrigin = "http://localhost:8001";

function allowCors(request, response) {
  response.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", allowedOrigin);
  if (request.method === "OPTIONS") {
    response.writeHead(204, {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Methods": "GET, POST",
      "Access-Control-Allow-Headers": "Content-Type",
      "Access-Control-Max-Age": "600",
    });
    response.end();
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}

const picker = document.querySelector(".theme-picker");
const root = document.documentElement;

function applyTheme(theme) {
  if (theme) {
    root.dataset.theme = theme;
  } else {
    root.removeAttribute("data-theme");
  }
}

const saved = localStorage.getItem("theme") || "";
console.log(`Saved theme: ${saved || "none"}`);
picker.value = saved;
applyTheme(saved);

picker.addEventListener("change", () => {
  applyTheme(picker.value);
  localStorage.setItem("theme", picker.value);
});

const tasks = [
  { id: 1, text: "Pack water", done: false },
  { id: 2, text: "Charge phone", done: true },
];

localStorage.setItem("tasks", JSON.stringify(tasks));

const stored = localStorage.getItem("tasks");
console.log(typeof stored);
console.log(stored);

const loaded = JSON.parse(stored);
console.log(loaded[1].text, loaded[1].done);
console.log(localStorage.getItem("no-such-key"));

document.cookie = "seen-tour=yes; Max-Age=31536000; Path=/; SameSite=Lax";
document.cookie = "units=km; Max-Age=31536000; Path=/; SameSite=Lax";

console.log(document.cookie);

document.cookie = "seen-tour=; Max-Age=0; Path=/";
console.log(document.cookie);

const url = new URL("https://shop.example.com/search?q=red+shoes&size=9#reviews");

console.log(url.hostname);
console.log(url.pathname);
console.log(url.searchParams.get("q"));
console.log(url.searchParams.get("size"));
console.log(url.hash);

url.searchParams.set("q", "trail shoes & socks");
url.searchParams.delete("size");
console.log(url.href);

const filter = document.querySelector(".filter");

const params = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
filter.value = params.get("q") || "";
console.log(`Filter on load: ${filter.value || "none"}`);

filter.addEventListener("input", () => {
  const url = new URL(location.href);
  if (filter.value) {
    url.searchParams.set("q", filter.value);
  } else {
    url.searchParams.delete("q");
  }
  history.replaceState(null, "", url);
});

const heading = document.querySelector(".view");

function show(view) {
  heading.textContent = `Showing the ${view}`;
  console.log(`${heading.textContent} (${location.search || "no query"})`);
}

document.querySelector("nav").addEventListener("click", (event) => {
  const link = event.target.closest("a");
  if (!link) {
    return;
  }
  event.preventDefault();
  history.pushState(null, "", link.href);
  show(link.dataset.view);
});

window.addEventListener("popstate", () => {
  const view = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get("view") || "list";
  show(view);
});

show(new URLSearchParams(location.search).get("view") || "list");

steps.body:
The browser has many more APIs than one lesson can teach in depth. This part is a map of the ones you're most likely to need, with what each one is for, so you know what to look up when a project calls for it. MDN, the reference site from the How the Web Works lessons, at [developer.mozilla.org](https://developer.mozilla.org), documents every one of them, and shows which browsers support each.

## Timers

`setTimeout(callback, ms)` runs a function once, later, and returns a number you can pass to `clearTimeout` to cancel it. `setInterval(callback, ms)` runs one again and again, until `clearInterval`. A common use is **debouncing**: waiting until someone stops typing for a moment before searching, instead of sending a request on every keystroke, as the first example shows.

`requestIdleCallback(callback)` runs a function when the browser has nothing else to do, which suits work that can wait, like sending analytics. Safari doesn't have it yet, so check with `if ("requestIdleCallback" in window)` before using it.

## Observers

Observers call your function when something about the page changes, without your code checking over and over:

- **IntersectionObserver** tells you when an element comes onto the screen or leaves it. It's how pages load more results as you scroll to the end, start a video when it scrolls into view, or record that someone saw an ad.
- **ResizeObserver** tells you when an element's size changes, for example to redraw a chart to fit its box.
- **MutationObserver** tells you when the DOM changes. Your own code rarely needs it, since you know when you change things, but it helps when watching a widget someone else wrote.
- **matchMedia** runs a media query from the CSS lessons in JavaScript: `matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches` is `true` in dark mode, and its `change` event fires when that changes.

## The clipboard, notifications, and permissions

`navigator.clipboard.writeText(text)` copies text, for a Copy button, and returns a promise. It works only on HTTPS or localhost, and usually only in response to a click.

`Notification.requestPermission()` asks to show notifications, and many other APIs, like the camera or the location, ask permission too. The browser shows the question, and if the person says no, you can't ask again. So ask only when someone has just asked for the feature, like clicking Notify me, never as the page loads. On iPhones, notifications only work for sites added to the home screen.

## Live connections

`fetch` asks and gets one answer. Some pages need the server to send things as they happen:

- **WebSockets**, with `new WebSocket("wss://...")`, keep a two-way connection open, so either side can send at any moment. Chat, multiplayer games, and documents edited by several people at once use them.
- **Server-sent events**, with `new EventSource("/api/updates")`, keep a one-way stream open from the server over plain HTTP. They suit live scores, notifications, and progress bars, and they reconnect by themselves if the connection drops.

## Workers

A **Web Worker** runs a script on another thread, so heavy work doesn't freeze the page: the long tasks from the previous lesson, moved out of the way. A worker can't touch the DOM. It and the page send each other messages with `postMessage`, and each listens for `message` events. This is `primes-worker.js`, which the last example starts:

```javascript
function isPrime(n) {
  for (let d = 2; d * d <= n; d++) {
    if (n % d === 0) {
      return false;
    }
  }
  return true;
}

self.addEventListener("message", (event) => {
  let count = 0;
  for (let n = 2; n < event.data; n++) {
    if (isPrime(n)) {
      count++;
    }
  }
  self.postMessage(count);
});
```

Inside a worker, `self` is the worker's own global object, the way `window` is the page's.

A **service worker** is a different kind of worker: a script that sits between your pages and the network, for your whole origin. It can answer requests from the Cache API, from the previous part, so a site keeps working offline, and it can receive push notifications when no tab is open. Service workers are what make a **PWA**, a progressive web app, a site that can be installed like an app. They need HTTPS, and they're tricky to update correctly, so most teams use a library like Workbox to write them.

So far, every file you've written has gone to the browser as it is. The next lesson shows how real projects install shared code with npm and bundle it with Vite.

